  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">CONSERVING THE CRITICAL ASIAN ELEPHANT POPULATIONS OF HUAI KHA KHAENG AND KAENG KRACHAN WORLD HERITAGE SITES THROUGH IMPROVED SMART PATROL AND HUMAN-ELEPHANT CONFLICT MITIGATION, YEAR 16-18</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The goal of this project is to recover Asian elephants in Thailands Western Forest Complex landscape through effective protection and collaborative human-elephant conflict (HEC) mitigation in World Heritage Sites of Huai Kha Khaeng National Park (HKK) and Kaeng Krachan National Park (KKNP). Project objectives are to: (1) strengthen the SMART (Spatial Monitoring and Reporting Tool) Patrol System in HKK and KKNP through training, technical support, and monitoring performance; (2) reduce HEC by promoting standard mitigation methods along with monitoring the effectiveness of those methods to examine temporal and spatial changes in elephant crop-raiding behavior; (3) estimate elephant population size in HKK and KKNP; and (4) expand conservation education programs to promote elephant conservation and HEC mitigation schemes to local communities, and improve cooperation among park staff, local communities, and other stakeholders. Project activities are to: (1) conduct follow-up monitoring with 43 patrol teams (5-6 rangers/team) per year in HKK and KKNP and provide technical support, refresher training, necessary equipment, and activities to support morale; (2) train 65 park rangers per year; (3) setup a new SMART Patrol Center in a new building at KKNP to improve its capacity and operation; (4) maintain an existing semi.permanent HEC mitigation fence; (5) monitor HEC effectiveness at HKK and KKNP; (6) develop and promote a real-time HEC warning system to support night response teams; (7) provide necessary equipment, fuel, and ration costs for three HEC night.response teams; (8) use DISTANCE program sampling and dung counts in HKK and KKNP to determine elephant population sizes; (9) conduct nature education campaigns for 15 schools reaching 600 students; and (10) improve and operate the Elephant Conservation Information Center providing updated facilities and information to all visitors and stakeholders.</narrative>
  </description>
